# The CSV fixtures are byte fixtures. Their line endings are part of what they
# test, not an artifact of whoever last saved them.
#
# Without this, core.autocrlf=true stores them LF-normalized, so the CRLF that
# prisma/fixtures/README.md documents as a deliberate hazard survives only in a
# Windows working copy. A fresh clone on Linux — CI, or a Vercel build — gets
# LF, and the parser is then verified against a file shaped differently from the
# one the README describes.
#
# `-text` means no conversion in either direction: the bytes committed are the
# bytes checked out, everywhere.
prisma/fixtures/*.csv -text
fixtures/sample-headers.csv -text

# Source is always text, whatever git's content heuristic makes of it.
#
# lib/roster.ts was once committed as "Bin 0 -> 7884 bytes" because a single
# stray NUL byte got into it: git treats a NUL in the first 8000 bytes as
# binary, and a binary file cannot be diffed or reviewed. The byte is gone and
# nothing in the repo carries another, but the failure was silent — the commit
# looked normal until someone ran `git show --stat` — and it costs one line to
# make it impossible rather than merely absent.
#
# Two attributes, doing two different jobs. `text` normalizes line endings on
# checkin. `diff` is the one that forces a textual diff — without it git falls
# back to sniffing the blob, so the roster.ts diff would render as binary
# forever on account of its parent blob, which still holds the NUL byte.
*.ts text diff
*.tsx text diff
*.md text diff
